      "entryId":"ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0071","sequence":83,
      "meaning":"After speaking, Nārada goes to Brahmā’s abode. Alone with her husband, Mainā says she did not understand the sage’s words. If the groom, home and family are incomparable, they should marry their daughter suitably; otherwise, dearer than life, Umā may remain unmarried. If no worthy groom is found, people will naturally call the mountain insensible. Her husband must arrange a marriage that will not burn their hearts afterward. She falls at his feet and lays down her head. Affectionately, the mountain lord replies that fire might sooner appear within the moon than Nārada’s word prove otherwise. His beloved should abandon every worry and remember the glorious Lord: the one who created Pārvatī will himself secure her welfare.",
