    {
      "entryId":"ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0072","sequence":84,
      "meaning":"The mountain king tells Mainā that, if she loves their daughter, she should instruct her to undertake the austerity by which Maheśa may be attained, for no other means will end the hardship. Nārada’s words are pregnant with reason: Vṛṣaketu is lovely and a treasury of every virtue. Understanding this, Mainā should abandon fear; Śaṅkara is stainless in every way. Rejoicing at her husband’s counsel, she rises and goes at once to Girijā. At the sight of Umā her eyes fill with tears; she seats the girl lovingly in her lap and repeatedly embraces her, unable to speak through her choked throat. The omniscient Mother of the world gently comforts her own mother: she says she saw a dream in which a handsome, fair and excellent Brahmin gave her this instruction.",
