    {
      "entryId":"ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0074","sequence":86,
      "meaning":"Holding her life-Lord’s feet in her heart, Umā goes into the forest and begins austerity. Her very tender body is unfit for ascetic hardship, yet remembering her husband’s feet she abandons every pleasure. Ever-new love for those feet arises daily; she forgets her body and fixes her mind on austerity. For a thousand years she eats roots and fruit, then passes a hundred years eating greens. For some days she lives on water and air and for some practices severe fasting. For three thousand years she eats dry bilva leaves fallen upon the ground. Then she gives up even dry leaves and so receives the name Aparṇā, ‘leafless.’ Seeing Umā’s body emaciated by austerity, a profound divine voice sounds from the sky: her heart’s desire has borne fruit, and she should abandon all unbearable hardship, for Tripurāri will now meet her.",
